The '''1998 United States Senate elections''' were held on November 3, 1998, with the 34 seats of [[Classes of United States senators|Class 3]] contested in regular elections. This was seen as an even contest between the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] and [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]]. While the Democrats had to defend more seats up for election, the Republican attacks on the [[morality]] of President [[Bill Clinton]] failed to connect with voters and the anticipated Republican gains did not materialize. The Republicans picked open seats up in [[#Ohio|Ohio]] and [[#Kentucky|Kentucky]] and narrowly defeated Democratic incumbent [[Carol Moseley Braun]] ([[#Illinois|Illinois]]), but these were cancelled out by the Democrats' gain of an open seat in [[#Indiana|Indiana]] and defeats of Republican Senators [[Al D'Amato]] ([[#New York|New York]]) and [[Lauch Faircloth]] ([[#North Carolina|North Carolina]]). The balance of the Senate remained unchanged at 55–45 in favor of the Republicans, thereby tying these election results with those of [[1996 United States Senate elections|1996]] and [[2004 United States Senate elections|2004]] as the highest number of Senate seats that the Republicans have held since [[1920 United States Senate elections|1920]].

This is the last election cycle that resulted in no net change in the balance of power and the last time Democrats won a U.S. Senate race in [[1998 United States Senate election in South Carolina|South Carolina]]. As of 2026, this is the most recent time in which the Democratic Party won a majority of [[Classes of United States senators|Class 3]] Senate seats in a regularly scheduled election.

