Incumbent Republican [[Al D'Amato]] was running for re-election to a fourth term, but lost to [[Chuck Schumer]] in what was considered by many to be the "high[est] profile and nastiest" contest of the year.<ref name=cnn>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ALLPOLITICS/stories/1998/11/03/election/senate/new.york/ | work=CNN | title =Schumer topples D'Amato in New York Senate race}}</ref>

[[Geraldine Ferraro]], former U.S. Representative and nominee for [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] in [[1984 United States presidential election|1984]], was well known for having been the [[1984 United States presidential election|1984 Democratic vice presidential nominee]] and had also run but lost in the Democratic primary in the [[1992 United States Senate election in New York|1992 U.S. Senate election in New York]]. [[Mark Green (New York politician)|Mark Green]], [[New York City Public Advocate]], had been the Democratic nominee in the [[1986 United States Senate election in New York|1986 election]], but lost in the general election to D'Amato.

At the start of 1998, Ferraro had done no fundraising, out of fear of conflict of interest with her job hosting the [[CNN]] program ''[[Crossfire (U.S. TV program)|Crossfire]]'', but was nonetheless perceived as the front-runner by virtue of her name recognition;<ref name="nyt-98ann">{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/1998/01/04/nyregion/friends-say-ferraro-will-seek-d-amato-s-seat.html | title = Friends Say Ferraro Will Seek D'Amato's Seat | author = Nagourney, Adam | author-link = Adam Nagourney | work = [[The New York Times]] | date=January 4, 1998}}</ref> indeed, December and January polls had her 25&nbsp;percentage points ahead of Green in the race and even further ahead of Schumer.<ref name="nyt-farewell">{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/1998/09/17/nyregion/the-1998-campaign-the-farewell-for-ferraro-early-promise-lopsided-loss.html | title =The Farewell: For Ferraro, Early Promise, Lopsided Loss | author=Waldman, Amy | work = [[The New York Times]] | date=September 17, 1998}}</ref><ref>{{cite book | last=Schumer | first=Chuck | author-link=Chuck Schumer | title=Positively American: Winning Back the Middle-Class Majority One Family at a Time | publisher=[[Rodale Books]] | year=2007 | isbn=978-1-59486-572-5 | url-access=registration | url=https://archive.org/details/positivelyameric00schu }} p. 17.</ref> Unlike her previous campaigns, Ferraro's family finances never became an issue in 1998.<ref name="nyt-farewell"/> However, she lost ground during the summer, with Schumer catching her in the polls by early August and then soon passing her.<ref>Schumer, ''Positively American'', p. 31.</ref> Schumer, a tireless fundraiser, outspent her by a five-to-one margin, and Ferraro failed to establish a political image current with the times.<ref name="nyt-farewell"/><ref>Schumer, ''Positively American'', pp. 18, 30.</ref> In the September 15, 1998, primary, she was beaten soundly by Schumer with a 51&nbsp;percent to 26&nbsp;percent margin.<ref name="nyt-farewell"/> Unlike the bitter 1992 Democratic senatorial primary, this contest was not divisive, and Ferraro and third-place finisher Green endorsed Schumer at a unity breakfast the following day.<ref>Schumer, ''Positively American'', pp. 33, 39.</ref>

The primaries were held on September 15, 1998.
